{"authored" [{"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^t(1+e^t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "603e98ecfe3f5ab511fab1a77205eafb2ff642204ebcffaff1f599383c4f5fa8" "basis" 2257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-334937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${Q}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nQ(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+0+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa159b8b2c2e0a63b17acc0ba599ae23e7a7523b9966d106ff2f86401129fcd"} {"question" "q-334937" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334937" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4t^2+12t^5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa159b8b2c2e0a63b17acc0ba599ae23e7a7523b9966d106ff2f86401129fcd"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+e^t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa159b8b2c2e0a63b17acc0ba599ae23e7a7523b9966d106ff2f86401129fcd"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^t+2e^{2t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa159b8b2c2e0a63b17acc0ba599ae23e7a7523b9966d106ff2f86401129fcd"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa159b8b2c2e0a63b17acc0ba599ae23e7a7523b9966d106ff2f86401129fcd"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "d8ac8ef8e4c29104c42f63039891c044cc55ab590dcab14b0a7aff6a12ba9f2d"} {"question" "